architecture-decision-record:
  identifier: "sheni_01"
  title: Primitive type set, spelling, and literal forms for Sheni
  status: accepted
  date: "2026-08-28"
  deciders:
    - felix
  superseded-by: ""
  context: |
    SYON parses; it does not interpret. A successful parse hands back
    Value::Scalar(String) -- the text between the delimiters, unchanged and
    untyped. That is deliberate (see ADR 0001's framing of SYON as safe and
    free of arbitrary type coercion), but it leaves every consumer to decide
    on its own what "42", "no", and "2026-08-28" mean.

    Sheni is the second layer: the type system that answers that question.
    Its README sketches four groups -- primitives, simple, complex,
    collections -- but the three later groups are all defined in terms of the
    first. A simple type is an interpretation laid over a primitive carrier
    (an id is an integer or a string, a date is a string in a fixed shape).
    An enum binds its values to a primitive. A collection's positions and
    keys are primitives. Nothing above the primitives can be pinned down
    until the primitives are.

    The layer 1 parser feeds strings to level 2, so the Norway problem of
    YAML is not a problem for the parser itself.
    Layer 2 knows about types and can therefore distinguish between a boolean `no`
    and the language code `no`.
    Additionally keys do always use a `string` primitive type.
    The norway problem is really an automatic conversion problem without knowing
    about the connected schema.
  decision: |
    Model the four groups as a single TypeGroup enum (Primitive, Simple,
    Complex, Collection). Every type descriptor in every group reports which
    group it belongs to, so the grouping is a property of the type system
    rather than a comment in a README.

    The primitive group holds exactly seven kinds: boolean, unsigned integer,
    signed integer, float, byte character, unicode character, and string.
    Integers and floats carry their width as part of the type rather than as
    a separate attribute -- there is no width-less "int" or "number". A value
    whose range is not stated is a value whose overflow behaviour is not
    stated, which is the class of bug this layer exists to prevent.

    Primitives are spelled the way Rust spells them: bool, u8 u16 u32 u64
    u128, i8 i16 i32 i64 i128, f32 f64, byte, char, string. Inventing new
    names for types the host language already names well buys nothing and
    costs every reader a lookup.

    String is a primitive, but is the one who most ofen is lifted to the simpple types
    (adding a functional interpretation) as noted in the the README's outline.
    The simple types are interpretations layered over a carrier, and most of them carry text;
    the carrier has to exist in the layer below the thing carried.

    Literal forms are a closed set, checked rather than guessed:

      - Booleans accept true, false, yes, and no, case-insensitively. on and
        off are reserved and rejected for now, as the README marks them
        "maybe later"; reserving them means adding them later cannot change
        the meaning of a document that parses today.
      - Integers are decimal digits with an optional leading minus on signed
        types. No leading plus, no digit separators, and no leading zeros
        except the single digit 0 -- a leading zero is octal in too many
        neighbouring languages to be worth the ambiguity.
      - Floats are finite decimal literals with an optional exponent. NaN and
        the infinities are rejected: they have no agreed text form across
        formats and do not survive a round trip.
      - A char is exactly one Unicode scalar value. A byte is one Unicode
        scalar whose code point fits in 0..=255.
      - A string accepts any well-formed UTF-8 text unchanged, including text
        that would also be a valid boolean or number. Reading "no" as a
        boolean happens only where the declared type is boolean.

    Reading a literal is fallible and reports why it failed. Errors carry a
    three-digit numeric code, banded by group --
    - 001-099 general,
    - 101-199 primitives,
    - 201-299 simple,
    - 301-399 complex,
    - 401-499 collections
    -- which is the same discipline ADR 0008 established for parse errors,
    applied to the layer above. As there, the code is API and the message
    wording is not.
  consequences:
    positive:
      - The norway problem cannot occur. A scalar is read as a boolean only where the schema declares a boolean, so an unquoted country code stays a string.
      - Widths being part of the type means an out-of-range literal is a typed error at read time rather than a silent wrap or a precision loss discovered later.
      - Borrowing Rust's spelling makes the type names self-documenting for the implementation language, and the mapping to the parsed representation is one to one with no lookup table.
      - Reserving on and off rather than accepting them keeps the option open without making today's documents ambiguous tomorrow.
    negative:
      - Requiring an explicit width is more verbose than a bare int, and every schema author pays that cost on every numeric field.
      - Rejecting leading zeros makes zero-padded data (a fixed-width numeric id, a legacy export) fail as an integer, forcing it to be declared a string. That is the correct reading, but it will surprise people.
      - A closed literal set means every extension is a new decision and a new ADR. That is the point, but it is friction.
      - The banded code space commits Sheni to a number never being reused for a different meaning, exactly as ADR 0008 committed the parser.
  alternatives-rejected:
    -
      name: A flat enum with one variant per width (U8, U16, U32, ...)
      reason: Combinatorially larger and it hides that width is a property of a number rather than a different kind of thing. Matching on "is this an integer" would have to enumerate every width.
    -
      name: Width-less int and float, defaulting to 64 bits
      reason: The convenient default is exactly the silent coercion this project rejects. A default width is still a width, just one nobody wrote down.
    -
      name: Accept YAML 1.1's full boolean vocabulary (y, n, yes, no, on, off, true, false, and their case variants)
      reason: That vocabulary is the origin of the Norway problem. The closed set here is narrower and, more importantly, only ever consulted where a boolean was declared.
    -
      name: Reuse serde_json::Value as the value model
      reason: It has one number type, loses width and signedness, and cannot represent a byte character or a distinction between char and a one-character string. It is a serialization shape, not a type system.
    -
      name: Defer string to both the primitives group and as a core storage for simple groups, as the README's outline reads
      reason: Every simple type is an interpretation of a carrier. Without string in the primitive group, the group the simple types are built on would be missing the carrier most of them use.
